  "session_1775159078485": {
    "messages": [
      {
        "role": "user",
        "content": "A student has finished their 4th semester. They have earned 30 credits from their first year and 25 credits from their second year. Will they be promoted to the 5th semester? Explain the specific credit deficit based on the 108/80/60 rule."
      },
      {
        "role": "assistant",
        "content": "No. The student will **not be promoted to the 5th semester**.\n\n**Why – based on the 108 / 80 / 60 rule**\n\n| Requirement for promotion to Semester 5/6 | Required | Student’s earned |\n|--------------------------------------------|----------|------------------|\n| **Total earned credits** (by end of Semester IV) | 60 credits | 55 credits |\n| **Credits from the first two semesters** | 36 credits | 30 credits |\n\n- **Total credit deficit:** 5 credits short of the required 60.  \n- **First‑year credit deficit:** 6 credits short of the required 36 from Sem 1 + Sem 2.\n\nBecause both the overall credit minimum (60) **and** the minimum first‑year credit requirement (36) are not met, the student fails the promotion criteria for moving into Semester 5 (or 6).  \n\n(For reference, promotion to Sem 7/8 later requires 108 total credits, with at least 80 from the first four semesters.)"
      },
      {
        "role": "user",
        "content": "If this is their third time being 'Not Promoted,' what is their last resort to stay in the program, and what is the absolute maximum time they have to graduate?"
      },
      {
        "role": "assistant",
        "content": "**Last‑resort option:**  \nAfter the third “Not Promoted” status the student’s name is removed from the faculty rolls. The only way to stay in the program is to file a **Mercy Appeal to the Vice‑Chancellor**. If the appeal is approved, the university may extend the allowed study period.\n\n**Absolute maximum time to graduate:**  \n- Normal ceiling: **14 consecutive semesters** (the standard maximum).  \n- With a successful Mercy Appeal: the period can be stretched by **up to two additional semesters**, giving an **absolute maximum of 16 semesters** (i.e., eight years)."
      },
      {
        "role": "user",
